      "title": "Use the tax year and the combined limit",
      "narration": "For tax year twenty twenty-six, the general combined limit for regular traditional and Roth I R A contributions is seven thousand five hundred dollars, or qualifying compensation if that is less. Someone age fifty or older can have an additional eleven hundred dollar catch-up, making the general dollar limit eighty-six hundred dollars. Suppose Marcus is forty-five and has only four thousand dollars of qualifying compensation, with no spousal rule. His regular contribution limit is four thousand dollars. If he has enough compensation and contributes three thousand dollars to a traditional account, that generally leaves forty-five hundred dollars of the combined annual limit, before checking Roth income restrictions.",
