      "title": "Being allowed to contribute does not establish a deduction",
      "narration": "A traditional I R A contribution may be fully deductible, partly deductible or nondeductible. Workplace retirement coverage, filing status and modified adjusted gross income affect the deduction rules. Being covered by an employer plan does not by itself prohibit a regular traditional contribution. It can instead limit the deduction. If neither spouse is covered by a workplace plan, those income phaseouts for deducting contributions do not apply, although contribution limits still do. A permitted nondeductible contribution must be tracked as basis, generally using Form eighty-six oh six. Keep that record so after-tax money is not taxed again. Contribution eligibility and deduction eligibility are separate exam questions.",
