      "title": "Tax deferral is followed by distribution rules",
      "narration": "Growth in a traditional I R A generally is not taxed annually while it stays inside the arrangement. If it contains only deductible contributions and untaxed earnings, an ordinary distribution generally is fully taxable income. Buying an annuity inside that account does not create an after-tax investment that never existed. If nondeductible amounts are present, the required allocation can make distributions partly taxable; the owner cannot simply choose to withdraw only basis from one account. A taxable six thousand dollar distribution at age forty-five, with no exception, generally also produces six hundred dollars of additional federal tax. An exception to that additional tax does not automatically remove ordinary income tax.",
